---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';

const paste = 'Set up cooked for me: clone https://github.com/dihannahdi/cooked into a temp folder and follow its SKILL.md';
---
<Base
  title="Let it cook · Dihan Nahdi"
  description="I used to keep checking on Claude Code while it worked. A small sound let me stop, and spend the wait learning something else."
>
  <article class="wrap essay">
    <p class="eyebrow">Why I made cooked · 23 Nov 2026</p>
    <h1>Let it cook</h1>
    <p class="essay__lede">A small sound that let me stop watching Claude work.</p>

    <p>When I give Claude Code a task, it usually works for a few minutes. Sometimes ten, sometimes more.</p>
    <p>
      I used to spend those minutes checking. Switch to the terminal. Still working. Switch back. A minute later, check
      again. Nothing was wrong, and nothing needed me. I just couldn’t stop wondering if it was done yet.
    </p>
    <p>
      It was a small anxiety, but it stayed with me all day. I would open a course and not really follow it. I would start
      reading for some research, then stop after one paragraph to look at the terminal again. The waits were short, but
      because I kept checking, I never really left.
    </p>

    <h2>A sound instead of a glance</h2>
    <p>
      So I made Claude Code tell me. Claude Code can run a small script at fixed moments, called <a
        href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks" rel="noopener">hooks</a>. I added one that plays a sound and shows a
      Windows notification when Claude is done, and when it needs my permission or an answer. The sound is one I picked
      myself, so I know it’s Claude without looking.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/skills/cooked-toasts.png"
        width="534"
        height="373"
        alt="Two Windows notifications from Claude Code. One says a session is cooked after 4 minutes 12 seconds. The other says the session needs your ok to run Bash."
      />
    </figure>
    <p>
      Now I give Claude a task and turn away from it. I open the course, or the paper, or whatever I wanted to learn that
      day. If there is no sound, it is still cooking and nothing needs me. When the sound comes, I go back.
    </p>
    <p>That was the real change for me. Silence used to mean “go check”. Now it means “keep going”.</p>

    <h2>What five days looked like</h2>
    <p>
      In its first five days it called me back 593 times. Each one is a moment I used to go and look for myself, usually
      more than once.
    </p>
    <p>
      It also kept quiet 523 times, for quick replies under 30 seconds and when I was already looking at that tab. A ping I
      didn’t need would have pulled me right back into checking.
    </p>
    <p>
      Claude doesn’t need me to watch it work. It needs me when it’s done, or when it’s stuck. The time in between is mine
      again.
    </p>

    <h2>Try it</h2>
    <p>
      If you use Claude Code on Windows, you can set it up with one paste. It asks you four things (voice, sound, when to
      ping you, and whether to push to your phone), then sends a test notification so you see it working. It’s Windows only
      for now, because it uses Windows notifications.
    </p>
    <pre class="essay__paste"><code>{paste}</code></pre>
    <ul class="links">
      <li><a class="btn btn--primary" href="/skills/cooked/">How cooked works</a></li>
      <li><a class="btn" href="https://github.com/dihannahdi/cooked" rel="noopener">View on GitHub</a></li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .essay { padding: 72px 0 0; }
  .essay > * { max-width: 64ch; }
  .essay p { font-size: 18px; line-height: 1.7; margin: 0 0 18px; }
  .essay__lede { font-size: clamp(19px, 2.4vw, 22px) !important; color: var(--muted); margin: 12px 0 36px !important; }
  .essay h2 { font-size: 22px; font-weight: 800; margin: 44px 0 14px; }
  .essay a:not(.btn) { color: var(--accent); }
  .essay figure { margin: 28px 0; }
  .essay img { width: 100%; max-width: 534px; height: auto; }
  .essay__paste {
    margin: 8px 0 0; padding: 16px; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius); background: var(--surface);
    white-space: pre-wrap; word-break: break-word; font: 500 14px/1.7 ui-monospace, 'SFMono-Regular', Menlo, Consolas, monospace;
  }
  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .essay { padding-top: 40px; }
    .essay p { font-size: 17px; }
  }
</style>
